  {
    "question": "For a class XI dropper studying functions, which statement about bijective functions is correct?",
    "options": {
      "A": "A bijective function exists only when the domain and codomain have the same number of elements.",
      "B": "A bijective function may map a single element of the domain to multiple elements of the codomain.",
      "C": "The inverse of a bijective function is not necessarily a function.",
      "D": "Every bijective function is surjective but not necessarily injective."
    },
    "correct_answer": "A",
    "explanation": "A bijection requires both injectivity and surjectivity, which is possible only when the domain and codomain are of equal cardinality (m = n).",
